event: result
data: {"run_id": "20260930-040818-b559d0", "report": {"title": "What Is Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)?", "summary_claims": [{"text": "Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is an artificial intelligence framework that combines information retrieval systems with generative large language models.", "kind": "factual", "answer_slot": "definition", "evidence_ids": ["S3-e1"], "citation_ids": ["S3"]}, {"text": "RAG retrieves relevant external documents from a knowledge source and incorporates them into the generation process.", "kind": "factual", "answer_slot": "distinguishing_property", "evidence_ids": ["S6-e1"], "citation_ids": ["S6"]}], "key_findings": [], "sections": [], "contradictions": [], "limitations": ["The supplied evidence does not establish a comprehensive account of RAG\u2019s limitations or common failure modes.", "Only limited evidence was found for: What are RAG\u2019s main benefits, limitations, and common failure modes.", "1 extracted finding(s) were excluded because their quotes could not be located in the source text", "1 generated claim(s) were excluded because the cited evidence did not support them, or because verification did not reach them within this run's budget."]}, "plan": {"sub_questions": [{"id": "SQ1", "text": "What does retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) mean, and what problem is it designed to address?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ2", "text": "How does a typical RAG system retrieve information and use it to generate a response?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ3", "text": "What are the main components of a RAG system, such as the knowledge source, retrieval method, and language model?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ4", "text": "How does RAG differ from answering with a language model alone or using fine-tuning?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ5", "text": "What are RAG\u2019s main benefits, limitations, and common failure modes?", "is_followup": false}]}, "evidence": [{"id": "S6-e1", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG addresses reliance on a language model\u2019s internal knowledge by retrieving external documents and incorporating them into generation.", "quote": "Instead of relying solely on the model\u2019s internal knowledge, RAG retrieves relevant external documents from a knowledge source (e.g., databases, search engines, or vector stores) and incorporates them into the generation process.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q1", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S6-e2", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "A typical RAG workflow retrieves external knowledge and incorporates it into the query context used to inform the language model\u2019s generated output.", "quote": "By integrating retrieval mechanisms, RAG systems retrieve relevant external knowledge during the retrieval phase, which is then incorporated into the query. This ensures that the LLM\u2019s generated output is informed by up-to-date and contextually relevant information[16].", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e3", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The source describes RAG as having retrieval and generation phases, with retrieval using a retriever to fetch relevant documents for the input query.", "quote": "In the retrieval phase, a retriever fetches relevant documents based on the input query using three retrieval approaches: a sparse retriever leveraging (B\u200bM\u200b25BM25 [10]-based lexical matching), a dense retriever(using embeddings from Sentence Transformers), or a hybrid approach (combining both methods).", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.96, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e4", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The source identifies external knowledge sources such as databases, search engines, and vector stores as sources of documents for RAG.", "quote": "Instead of relying solely on the model\u2019s internal knowledge, RAG retrieves relevant external documents from a knowledge source (e.g., databases, search engines, or vector stores) and incorporates them into the generation process.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.9, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e5", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "RAG differs from relying on a standalone LLM by using external retrieval, which the source says can address fine-tuning costs, hallucinations, input-window constraints, and knowledge cutoffs.", "quote": "By integrating retrieval mechanisms from external sources, RAG effectively addresses major limitations of standalone LLMs [2, 3], including the high costs associated with training and fine-tuning [4], the issue of hallucination [5, 6, 7, 8], and constraints imposed by the input window [9] and knowledge cut-off[1].", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e6", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "The study reports that its hybrid retriever improved retrieval relevance and reliability while reducing hallucination rates.", "quote": "These findings highlight the hybrid retriever\u2019s ability to enhance retrieval relevance, reduce hallucination rates, and improve LLM reliability, emphasizing the importance of advanced retrieval techniques in mitigating hallucinations and improving response accuracy.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.95, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e1", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG is described as a method that retrieves supporting documents to mitigate hallucinations and factual inconsistencies from language models.", "quote": "Traditional retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) frameworks mitigate this issue by retrieving supporting documents; however, they typically rely on a single retrieval pass and lack mechanisms for evaluating answer consistency or handling conflicting evidence.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.94, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q1", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S5-e2", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "MEGA-RAG retrieves evidence using dense search, keyword search, and biomedical knowledge graphs, then generates and reranks candidate answers.", "quote": "First, the Multi-Source Evidence Retrieval (MSER) Module aggregates heterogeneous information through FAISS-based dense retrieval, BM25 keyword search, and biomedical knowledge graphs, thereby improving both recall and factual grounding.", "quote_match": "fuzzy", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.392, "citable": false, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e3", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "The system generates multiple candidate answers with an LLM and reranks them using cross-encoder relevance scores.", "quote": "Second, the Diverse Prompted Answer Generation (DPAG) Module generates multiple candidate answers via prompt-based LLM sampling, which are then reranked using cross-encoder relevance scoring (33).", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.96, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e4", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "MEGA-RAG\u2019s evidence sources include peer-reviewed biomedical literature such as trials, systematic reviews, and clinical practice guidelines.", "quote": "PubMed articles and abstracts: Retrieves peer-reviewed biomedical literature, including randomized controlled trials, systematic reviews, and clinical practice guidelines.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.91, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e5", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The system includes a biomedical knowledge graph that represents relationships among pathogens, interventions, host responses, and epidemiological outcomes.", "quote": "Utilizes a curated graph of biomedical triples (head, relation, tail) that encodes mechanistic links between pathogens, interventions, host responses, and epidemiological outcomes.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.92, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e6", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "Unlike a language model answering from internal knowledge alone, RAG retrieves supporting documents to reduce the risk of hallucinations and factual inconsistencies.", "quote": "As illustrated in Figures 1, 2, conventional large language models (LLMs) generate answers based solely on their internal knowledge, which increases the risk of hallucinations and factual inconsistencies.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.87, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S2-e1", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG retrieves relevant documents from a knowledge base and uses them as context for an LLM to generate a response.", "quote": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is an AI architecture that enhances Large Language Model (LLM) responses by first retrieving relevant documents from a knowledge base and then using those documents as context for the LLM\u2019s generation step.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S2-e2", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG is designed to address standalone LLMs\u2019 lack of access to specific organizational data that was not included in training.", "quote": "RAG addresses the core limitation of standalone LLMs \u2014 their knowledge is fixed at training time and doesn\u2019t include your specific organizational data.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S2-e3", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "A typical RAG workflow loads data into a source, retrieves relevant information for the query, and combines that information with the query in a prompt for generation.", "quote": "The RAG workflow involves data loading into a source like a vector database, retrieval of relevant data based on user query, and augmentation where retrieved data and user query are combined into a prompt.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S2-e4", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The source identifies a retriever, a generator, and a knowledge base as the three fundamental components of RAG.", "quote": "1. The Retriever: The intelligent search engine that finds relevant information\n 2. The Generator: The large language model that produces responses\n 3. The Knowledge Base: The vector database storing indexed information", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S2-e5", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "RAG differs from answering with a standalone language model by retrieving information from connected data sources instead of relying only on the model\u2019s pretrained knowledge.", "quote": "Instead of relying solely on the model\u2019s pre-trained knowledge, RAG retrieves relevant information from connected data sources and uses it to generate a more accurate and context-aware response.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.9, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S2-e6", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "The source presents improved contextual relevance and factual accuracy as benefits of RAG.", "quote": "RAG is a hybrid framework that integrates a retrieval mechanism with a generative model to improve the contextual relevance and factual accuracy of generated content.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.9, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e1", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG augments a language model with external knowledge to reduce hallucinations and provide access to information beyond its training cutoff.", "quote": "The previous chapter established why retrieval-augmented generation matters: it grounds language models in external knowledge, reducing hallucinations and letting access to information beyond the training cutoff.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e2", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "A typical RAG system retrieves relevant documents for a query, then has the generator use those documents to compose a response.", "quote": "The retriever acts as a search engine, filtering millions of documents to identify the few most relevant ones. The generator then reads these documents and synthesizes a response that addresses your question.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e3", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The two primary components are a retriever that searches a knowledge base and a generator, typically an LLM, that synthesizes an answer from retrieved documents.", "quote": "A RAG system consists of two primary components: a retriever that finds relevant documents from a knowledge base, and a generator (typically a large language model) that synthesizes an answer from those documents.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S1-e4", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Retrieval commonly involves document encoding, indexing, and query-time retrieval.", "quote": "In practice, achieving effective retrieval involves three sub-systems working together: document encoding, indexing, and query-time retrieval.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.91, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S1-e5", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "RAG can use retrieved information in place of some knowledge stored in the language model\u2019s parameters.", "quote": "The key insight was that retrieval could replace some of the knowledge stored in model parameters.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.78, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e6", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "RAG can reduce hallucinations and provide access to information beyond the model\u2019s training cutoff.", "quote": "The previous chapter established why retrieval-augmented generation matters: it grounds language models in external knowledge, reducing hallucinations and letting access to information beyond the training cutoff.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.9, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e1", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG combines information retrieval with generative language models to produce outputs grounded in external information.", "quote": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is an artificial intelligence framework that combines information retrieval systems with generative large language models (LLMs) to produce more accurate, up-to-date, and context-grounded outputs.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e2", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG addresses the problem that pretrained models may contain outdated or incomplete knowledge.", "quote": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation solves a core limitation of pre-trained models because pre-trained models store knowledge in parameters that can become outdated or incomplete.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e3", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "A typical RAG system embeds a query, retrieves relevant material, and passes the material to a language model to generate a grounded response.", "quote": "The retriever encodes user queries into vector representations and searches indexed knowledge bases, such as document stores or vector databases, to locate semantically relevant content. This retrieved context is then passed to the generator, typically a large language model, which synthesizes a coherent response grounded in that information.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q4", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S3-e4", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The main RAG components include a knowledge base, embedding model, retriever, vector database, and generator, with each handling a distinct role.", "quote": "The knowledge base stores source content, the embedding model converts content and queries into vectors, the retriever finds relevant matches, the vector database stores and indexes embeddings, and the generator produces the final answer.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S3-e5", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "Unlike fine-tuning or prompt engineering, RAG adds external knowledge at query time rather than relying only on changed prompts or model weights.", "quote": "RAG differs from prompt engineering and fine-tuning because RAG adds external knowledge at query time instead of relying only on better prompts or retrained model weights.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e6", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "RAG can support continuous knowledge updates without retraining, which the source presents as a benefit for scalability and cost.", "quote": "By separating knowledge storage from model weights, RAG systems support continuous updates without retraining, making them scalable and cost-efficient.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.93, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S4-e1", "source_id": "S4", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG connects an LLM to external knowledge at inference time so it can generate responses that go beyond its static training data.", "quote": "Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) is an AI architecture pattern that connects large language models to external knowledge sources at inference time, enabling those models to generate accurate, context-aware responses that go beyond their static training data.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S4-e2", "source_id": "S4", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "RAG is designed to address outdated answers and an LLM\u2019s inability to access proprietary or real-time information.", "quote": "LLMs often provide outdated answers due to knowledge cutoffs and cannot access proprietary internal documents or real-time external data sources.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S4-e3", "source_id": "S4", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "At query time, a RAG system embeds the user input, searches the vector store, and retrieves the top-k relevant document chunks.", "quote": "When a user submits a query, the RAG system applies the same embedding model to convert the user input into a vector representation and queries the vector store, executing a similarity search that returns the top-k most relevant document chunks.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q4", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S4-e4", "source_id": "S4", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "The retrieved context is added to the prompt, which the generator uses to produce the final response.", "quote": "The generator receives the augmented prompt and produces the final response.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q4", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S4-e5", "source_id": "S4", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "A RAG system includes a knowledge base, retriever, integration layer, and LLM generator.", "quote": "RAG systems contain four primary components: a knowledge base that stores external knowledge, an information retrieval component (the retriever) that finds relevant documents for each query, an integration layer that assembles retrieved context into an LLM prompt, and a generator (the LLM) that produces the final response.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S4-e6", "source_id": "S4", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "Unlike relying only on knowledge acquired during pretraining, RAG retrieves external documents for each query and places them in the LLM prompt.", "quote": "Rather than relying on knowledge encoded during pretraining, a RAG system retrieves relevant documents from an external database in response to each user query and injects that content into the LLM prompt before generation.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}], "sources": [{"id": "S1", "url": "https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/rag-architecture-retriever-generator-design-patterns", "title": "RAG Architecture: Components, Timing & Design Patterns - Interactive", "domain": "mbrenndoerfer.com", "source_type": "other", "content_origin": "provider_raw", "quality_score": 0.626, "page_count": null, "usable": true, "fetch_status": "provider_content"}, {"id": "S2", "url": "https://www.kernshell.com/anatomy-of-rag-retriever-generator-and-the-workflow", "title": "Anatomy of RAG: Retriever, Generator and Workflow Explained | 2025 Guide", "domain": "kernshell.com", "source_type": "other", "content_origin": "provider_raw", "quality_score": 0.633, "page_count": null, "usable": true, "fetch_status": "provider_content"}, {"id": "S3", "url": "https://searchatlas.com/blog/what-is-rag", "title": "What is RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): How It Works, Architecture & Implementation Guide", "domain": "searchatlas.com", "source_t