models. [S3] RAG retrieves relevant external documents from a knowledge source and incorporates them into the generation process. [S6]\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The supplied evidence does not establish a comprehensive account of RAG\u2019s limitations or common failure modes.\n- Only limited evidence was found for: What are RAG\u2019s main benefits, limitations, and common failure modes.\n- 1 extracted finding(s) were excluded because their quotes could not be located in the source text\n- 1 generated claim(s) were excluded because the cited evidence did not support them, or because verification did not reach them within this run's budget.\n\n## Sources\n\n- **[S1]** [RAG Architecture: Components, Timing & Design Patterns - Interactive](https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/rag-architecture-retriever-generator-design-patterns) \u2014 mbrenndoerfer.com, other, n.d., quality 0.63 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S2]** [Anatomy of RAG: Retriever, Generator and Workflow Explained | 2025 Guide](https://www.kernshell.com/anatomy-of-rag-retriever-generator-and-the-workflow) \u2014 kernshell.com, other, n.d., quality 0.63 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S3]** [What is RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): How It Works, Architecture & Implementation Guide](https://searchatlas.com/blog/what-is-rag) \u2014 searchatlas.com, blog, n.d., quality 0.58\n- **[S4]** [End-to-End RAG Workflow: How Retrieval Augmented Generation Works | Databricks Blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/rag-workflow) \u2014 databricks.com, blog, n.d., quality 0.58 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S5]** [MEGA-RAG: a retrieval-augmented generation framework with multi-evidence guided answer refinement for mitigating hallucinations of LLMs in public health](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12540348) \u2014 pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov, academic, n.d., quality 0.96 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S6]** [Hybrid Retrieval for Hallucination Mitigation in Large Language Models: A Comparative Analysis](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.05324v1) \u2014 arxiv.org, academic, n.d., quality 0.97\n\n## Citation verification\n\n- Evidence references: 2, 2 resolved to citable evidence (100%). Citation markers are derived from those references by the engine, so citation integrity is a structural invariant rather than a measurement.\n- Evidence-owing claims carrying a citation: 100% of 2\n- Entailment checked against each claim's own evidence, over every eligible claim: 2 supported, 0 partially supported, 1 unsupported, 0 not checked\n- Retrieved but never cited: S1, S2, S4, S5\n\n<details><summary>Open citation issues</summary>\n\n- `unsupported_claim` the claim asserts 2 things and 'A RAG system has a retriever, a generator' is not supported: best entailment 0.109 from S2-e4 is below the 0.98 support threshold \u2014 A RAG system has a retriever, a generator, and a knowledge base as its three fundamental components.\n\n</details>\n\n## Run metrics\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Mode | cloud |\n| Research rounds | 1 |\n| Stopped because | coverage judged sufficient |\n| Search queries | 6 |\n| Unique sources | 6 |\n| Usable sources | 6 |\n| Distinct domains | 6 |\n| Fetches avoided by dedup | 5 |\n| Evidence items | 36 |\n| Quotes verbatim (exact-normalised) | 97% |\n| Quotes fuzzy (excluded from citation) | 3% |\n| LLM calls | 13 |\n| Tokens (in/out) | 27,969 / 12,956 |\n| Estimated cost | $0.0093 (8 responses used a token category with no recorded rate) |\n| Duration | 119.1s |\n\n---\n\n_Generated by Agentic Research Engine on 2026-09-30 04:10 UTC._\n"}

